<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Sogaya</h1><p class="st-meta">200 CE – 562 CE (approx.) · capital Goseong</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Gaya state on the southern coast at modern Goseong, one of the six Gaya polities of the founding legend.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sogaya, meaning Little Gaya, grew up around the bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goseong-county-south-gyeongsang">Goseong</a> on the south coast. It is often identified with Goja-guk, one of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a> statelets named in Chinese records, and in legend its founder was one of the six brothers born from golden eggs in 42 CE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As a coastal polity it depended on fishing and sea trade along the southern shore. It is often identified with one of the eight port states that, according to Korean chronicles, attacked a neighbouring Gaya state in the early 3rd century and were repelled with Silla's help. Tombs at Songhak-dong show contacts with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/baekje">Baekje</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sogaya was absorbed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a> during its conquest of the remaining Gaya states around 562, becoming part of Silla's local administration.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 200 CE – 562 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Korea and Japan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:63.68%;width:7.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 CE – 562 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Goseong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaya language</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>So Gaya, Goja-guk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=381&amp;focus=sogaya" aria-label="Sogaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7918 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sogaya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8247 1162h0M8260 1139h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8238 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8238 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day South Korea. <a href="/atlas?year=381&amp;focus=sogaya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

